  <h1>Where these dates come from</h1>

  <div class="ab-stat"><b>{{ animalDays.days | length }}</b><span>animal days a year: <strong>{{ officialCount }}</strong> official awareness days and <strong>{{ pickCount }}</strong> Wild Atlas picks.</span></div>

  <h2>We didn't make them up</h2>
  <p>Every official day on the calendar was started by someone else — and different days come from different places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The United Nations</strong> declared some of them, like World Wildlife Day and World Oceans Day.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservation groups, zoos and aquariums</strong> started many more, to help people care about one animal at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Animal fans</strong> started a few. World Octopus Day, for example, began with an online community of octopus lovers in 2007.</li>
    <li>A few, like <strong>National Bison Day</strong>, are United States days, and some started with a government resolution.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Most are celebrated all over the world, on the same date every year. A few move around (like "the third Friday in May"), so we check those dates again each year.</p>

  <h2>How an animal gets on the calendar</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One animal a week</strong>, matched to a real awareness day.</li>
    <li>When several animal days land in the same week, we pick the one we think kids will love most.</li>
    <li>In the few weeks with no animal day, we choose a favourite and label it a <span class="pick-badge">Wild Atlas pick</span>.</li>
    <li>The year ends with <strong>Explorer's Choice</strong>, where families vote for a favourite.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What's on every animal's page</h2>
  <p>A short story to read out loud together, how big the animal is next to a five-year-old, and where you can see one for real. Every fact is checked against trusted sources, like the IUCN Red List and the zoos and aquariums themselves.</p>

